  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't seen the rule run yet: its status is still \"pending\".\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` uses `timestamp` as its time field. Its main fields are:\n- **Response code:** `response` / `response.keyword`, stored as text like \"503\", not as a number.\n- **Size:** `bytes`.\n- **Who and where:** `clientip`, `geo.src` / `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`.\n- **What was requested:** `url`, `request`, `extension`, `host`.\n- **Client details:** `agent`, `machine.os`, `machine.ram`, `referer`, `tags`.\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-4209449a Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-4209449a-web-logs-overview-2c0eb1d61efd`). It has 7 panels:\n- Total requests (metric)\n- Unique visitors, counted by distinct `clientip` (metric)\n- Total bytes served (metric)\n- Requests over time, split by response code (bar)\n- Share of each response code (pie)\n- Top 10 requested URLs with request count and average bytes (table)\n- Traffic by source country (bar)\n\n**3. Rule: \"EFF-4209449a 5xx spike\"** (id `b54a81b8-86c3-466a-a53a-2c4e60f44522`). It searches `kibana_sample_data_logs` every minute for requests whose response code starts with \"5\". It fires when more than 10 of them arrive within 5 minutes, measured on `timestamp`.\n\nI couldn't run a test search against the index because Kibana's console proxy rejected the API key. So I haven't confirmed the rule's query matches any documents. The match on codes starting with \"5\" assumes the sample data stores codes like \"503\" as text, which is how the field is set up.\n\nThe rule has no action connector attached. It will record alerts in Kibana but won't send notifications anywhere.",
